capture: startup-time asset pipeline
runtime: JDK 8
commit: c8f6226105
source: application log during container startup, asset-pipeline output relayed at DEBUG level
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: TWO preconditions were unmet, and they are named separately because
  they are different failures with different remedies.  Conflating them would
  leave a reader unable to tell why the region is empty, which is the precise
  error this field exists to prevent.
  (1) STACK UNAVAILABLE - there is no deployment to observe.  Measured, not
  assumed: the documented host name arkcase-ce.local does not resolve; there is
  no servlet-container installation on this host and no container launcher on
  the path; CATALINA_LOG, TOMCAT_HOME and CATALINA_HOME are all unset; no
  deployable web archive exists anywhere in the working tree; no container log
  exists anywhere on the filesystem; and the sibling notes/reference-stack.status
  records every reference service answering 000 with no response at all, with
  notes/readiness.status recording attempt-1=000.  The designated producer also
  refuses to start without an administrator credential supplied through its
  environment, and none is set here, so no run was possible even setting the
  absent deployment aside.
  (2) DEBUG NOT ENABLED FOR THE COPIER - a separate failure, and the one that
  would still bite with a container present.  The three pipeline invocations and
  every line of relayed pipeline output are emitted at DEBUG on the logger named
  after com.armedia.acm.userinterface.angular.AngularResourceCopier.  That
  logger's level is not fixed by anything in version control: the tracked file
  acm-standard-applications/arkcase/src/main/resources/log4j2-old.xml states in
  its own words that the logging configuration is externalised to
  $HOME/.arkcase/acm/log4j2.xml, and the tracked deployment descriptor
  acm-standard-applications/arkcase/src/main/webapp/META-INF/context.xml mounts
  exactly that external file over the web application's own logging
  configuration through a PreResources file-resource set - identically at commit
  c8f6226105 and in the current tree.  The level is therefore a deployment
  property, supplied from outside the repository.  Host observation, labelled as
  such and NOT a base-commit repository fact: the externalised configuration
  present on this host raises com.armedia to debug but then pins the copier's own
  logger to warn with additivity disabled, so under that stock configuration
  every About-to-run line, every relayed child line and every done-with line
  falls below threshold and is discarded before it reaches any appender.
  The older runtime is deliberately NOT listed among the unmet preconditions,
  because it is not one.  A JDK 8 installation is present here and the sibling
  env/toolchain.txt records its banner for this side of the capture.  What is
  missing is the deployment and the logging level, not the JVM.
exit-status-is-not-evidence: true
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is stated in
  full anyway, so the mirror reproduces the transformation rather than guessing
  at it.  When this region is captured the pipeline STRIPS timestamps and dates,
  thread names and thread identifiers, absolute filesystem paths in favour of a
  stable token, port assignments, elapsed-time and duration figures, and any
  progress-percentage churn.  It PRESERVES the behaviour-bearing content: task
  names, the order of the task graph, per-task file counts, the identity of each
  of the three commands, the logged exit codes - kept deliberately, as data, so
  a reader can see for themselves that they prove nothing - and the full message
  text of every warning and error line.  Over-normalising this region would
  destroy the very evidence it exists to carry.
  No colour-escape filter is applied, and none is needed.  Verified at commit
  c8f6226105: both task-runner commands carry the no-colour flag, at
  spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:29 and :37, so the relayed output contains
  no terminal escape sequences to strip.  The dependency-install command carries
  a no-progress flag at :34, which suppresses the progress churn on its side.
  Confirming an absence is the right response here; adding a filter for a
  condition that does not arise would be an unjustified change.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured, every
  credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder <REDACTED>,
  which is the convention this documentation tree already uses.  A dependency
  install is the specific carrier to watch, because it can echo a registry token
  or a remote URL with credentials embedded in its authority component; the scan
  therefore covers token assignments, authentication-configuration keys,
  password and secret assignments, and the user-and-password form of a URL
  authority, and every hit reads <REDACTED>.  The documented administrator
  credential is never transcribed here in any form, not even as an illustration
  of what redaction removes.
----- BEGIN CAPTURE -----
----- END CAPTURE -----
notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.  Because
  the two preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no log line
  was invented to fill the gap.  A fabricated baseline would make every
  behaviour-preserved claim in this migration unfalsifiable, which defeats the
  baseline-as-tie-breaker rule outright.  An explicit, machine-readable skip with
  a reason is rule-compliant; an invented capture is not, and is strictly worse
  than an honest absence.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across
  baseline and migrated are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They record an
  absence in both places.
- Resolving the empty-region ambiguity explicitly, rather than leaving it
  implicit.  Because this pipeline relays at DEBUG, an empty region is ambiguous
  between "the pipeline never ran" and "the pipeline ran and nothing was
  logged".  Here BOTH hold, and both are named in skip-reason: the pipeline
  never ran because there is no deployment, AND it would have logged nothing at
  the copier's stock level even if it had.  Leaving that ambiguity unresolved
  would be the same class of error the evidence rule below exists to prevent -
  an absence of output silently read as an absence of problems.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that the
  producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why the
  delimiters are empty.
- Region-selection rule, stated here so the migrated capture reproduces it
  without guesswork.  The producer mines the container log for this region with a
  fixed extended-regular-expression alternation, case-insensitively, and writes
  the matched lines under a line cap that it announces rather than applies
  silently.  The alternation, on one unbroken line so it can be reused verbatim:
  AngularResourceCopier|grunt|node_modules|package-lock
  The cap defaults to 2000 lines, and exceeding it marks the run incomplete.  The
  SAME pattern is used on both sides of the mirror, which is what makes the region
  boundary itself identical and the recursive diff mechanically meaningful; a
  pattern varied on one side only would register as a difference carrying no
  behavioural signal at all.
- Field-order rule for the mirror, also stated rather than left to inference.
  The field order is capture, runtime, commit, source, produced-by,
  region-status, then skip-reason when and only when the region is skipped, then
  exit-status-is-not-evidence, normalised and redacted.  skip-reason sits
  directly after the status it explains, matching the sibling
  startup/catalina.out.log so the two files in this directory parse the same
  way.  exit-status-is-not-evidence is specific to THIS region and is present on
  both sides regardless of status, because the claim it encodes is a property of
  the pipeline rather than of any particular run.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers ONLY the three startup-time asset-pipeline
  invocations and the lines immediately around them.  Container and application
  startup belong to the whole-window archive startup/catalina.out.log; context
  initialisation to the sibling context-initialisation region, which the archive
  refers to as startup/spring-context.log and the producer emits under its own
  region name context-init.log; the consolidated error-severity scan to the
  sibling scan, referred to as error-scan.txt and emitted by the producer under
  its region name errors.log; toolchain provenance to env/toolchain.txt; archived
  unit-test reports to surefire/; and the eight application flows to the flow
  captures.  Both names are given for those two siblings because the archive name
  and the producer's region name differ, and citing only one of the pair would
  leave a reader unable to match the file to the code that writes it.  None of
  them is duplicated here, and no digest is computed here.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic.  The repository's root ignore file uses bare, leading-slash-free
  patterns, and git matches those at ANY depth.  Verified at this directory's
  exact depth with a .log file placed inside each candidate: a directory named
  logs is ignored by root ignore rule 26, bin by rule 7, target by rule 6,
  builds by rule 40, activemq-data by rule 25 and partition-descriptor by rule
  24, while this file's own path is reported as not ignored and is therefore
  committable.  The ignored token is always the DIRECTORY name and never the
  .log extension, so a .log file placed inside any of those six directories
  would be silently uncommitted while every local check still passed.  This file
  therefore sits directly in startup/ and no subdirectory was created beneath
  it.  A directory named builds is the live temptation for a file about a build,
  and it is exactly one of the six.  The producer script guards the same trap
  from its own side, refusing to WRITE to a resolved path containing a forbidden
  segment.
- The pointed irony is worth recording.  The frontend's own dependency directory
  is node_modules, which the frontend's local ignore file - whose entire content
  is that one entry - keeps out of version control on purpose.  Reading FROM an
  ignored directory, or from a container's own log directory, is fine; writing
  INTO one is the trap, and it is why this archive directory is startup/.
- Governing rule on evidence, verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced
  artifacts and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is
  mandatory rather than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets
  grunt.option('force', true), which masks task failures and lets a broken build
  exit zero."  This region is that rule's primary evidence, which is why the
  header carries exit-status-is-not-evidence as a machine-readable field rather
  than burying the point in prose.
- The chain that rule describes was traced end to end through the actual code at
  commit c8f6226105, and it has five links.  ONE: Gruntfile.js:143-144 reads, in
  full, the comment "Making grunt default to force in order not to break the
  project." followed by grunt.option('force', true) - so a failing task does not
  fail the run.  TWO: the task runner therefore exits zero even when tasks fail.
  THREE: the Java side runs each command through the process executor's default
  implementation, which THROWS on a non-zero exit - so a zero exit means nothing
  is thrown.  FOUR: the helper logs done with [{}]: exit code {} at
  AngularResourceCopier.java:340 and startup CONTINUES.  FIVE: therefore a broken
  asset build logs exit code 0, raises nothing, and the deployment reaches ready
  state.
- Sharpening link THREE, verified by executing the pinned process-execution
  library rather than by reading its documentation.  The library version the
  reactor pins was exercised directly: a child exiting 0 makes the execute call
  RETURN 0 and throw nothing, while a child exiting non-zero makes the same call
  THROW.  The consequence is stronger than the rule needs to claim.  Because a
  non-zero exit throws before AngularResourceCopier.java:340 is ever reached, the
  ONLY exit code that line can EVER print is 0.  A logged exit code of 0 in this
  region is a tautology of the control flow, not a finding about the asset build.
  The same probe confirmed that the single-argument stream handler used at
  AngularResourceCopier.java:338 receives BOTH the child's standard output and
  its standard error, so a task's error output lands on the same DEBUG relay as
  its ordinary output and is subject to the same threshold.
- Stated plainly, because it is the whole point of this file: THE LOGGED EXIT
  CODE IS NOT EVIDENCE THAT THE ASSET BUILD SUCCEEDED.  THE PRODUCED ARTIFACTS
  ARE.  The five artifact digests for this side live in the sibling artifacts/
  directory, which is referenced here by directory name only; this region
  computes no digest and quotes no digest value.  A reader who wants to know
  whether the asset build worked must read that directory, not this one.
- Two pre-existing defects are masked by exactly that forced-execution setting,
  and they are recorded here as proof that the masking is real rather than
  theoretical.  Both were verified by reading Gruntfile.js at commit c8f6226105.
  FIRST: the alias registered at :379 as sync-dev runs concurrent:default, while
  the concurrent configuration block at :90-98 defines exactly one target, named
  default1 at :91.  The alias names a target that does not exist.  SECOND: the
  alias registered at :372 as lint runs a JavaScript linter followed by a
  stylesheet linter, but the JavaScript linter has NO configuration block
  anywhere in the file - its name occurs at :372 and nowhere else - whereas the
  stylesheet linter IS configured, at :40-47.
- Both aliases are OUTSIDE the default task graph, so NEITHER affects the five
  produced artifacts.  Saying so keeps the record accurate: their value is not
  that they break the build, it is that they demonstrate a real, in-tree path by
  which a broken frontend build exits zero.  Under the rule on discovered
  defects they are REGISTERED, NOT FIXED, and nothing in this file proposes,
  implies or performs a repair to either one.
- Independent corroboration from elsewhere in this evidence tree, cited with
  attribution so it is not mistaken for this region.  The sibling
  notes/frontend-comparison-provenance.txt records that when the base-commit
  pipeline was run for the artifact capture it exited ZERO while reporting a
  missing module-configuration file and the message that force was used and it
  was continuing.  That is the masking observed in a real run.  It was a
  standalone command-line run in a throwaway build location, with no container
  and no copier, so it is a DIFFERENT producer and a different territory: it is
  corroboration for the mechanism, not a capture of this region, and a reader
  must not extend it into one.
- Governing rule on discovered defects, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs discovered
  during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one blocks a
  validation item."  Applied to this region that means the two aliases above are
  registered and left alone, and every warning or error the pipeline emits is
  captured exactly as observed - nothing repaired, nothing retried into
  submission, nothing hidden.  The escape clause in that rule is invoked EXACTLY
  TWICE in the entire change set.  Both invocations are frontend-related, which
  is why they are worth naming as a boundary in this, the frontend region: one
  concerns a generated configuration module that the build requires and version
  control does not carry, the other the removal of a build package that cannot be
  compiled on the target runtime.  NEITHER invocation is in this folder, and
  nothing in this file performs, proposes or implies either of them.
- Governing rule on ambiguity, verbatim: "The application's observed behavior at
  the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and each
  resolution must be documented."
  [Editorial note: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase this
  documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution changes no meaning
  and is disclosed here rather than made silently.]
- That rule carries unusual weight for this side of the migration.  Verified at
  commit c8f6226105 and again in the current tree: the frontend contains ZERO
  specification files, despite declaring a test runner and an assertion library
  among its build dependencies.  There is therefore no automated behavioural test
  for the frontend to fall back on, and artifact-level identity against this
  captured baseline is the strongest behavioural evidence this side of the
  migration has.  That is also why an absence is recorded here instead of a
  plausible-looking substitute: an unrun pipeline's behaviour is unknowable
  rather than assumable.
- Governing rule on justified change: no dependency version changes without a
  specific compatibility reason for the target runtime.  Its CONTRAPOSITIVE is
  load-bearing and is recorded here deliberately, because it is the half most
  easily forgotten: HOLDING a version after assessment is as much a
  rule-compliant decision as changing one.  The task runner and its command-line
  interface were assessed by installing AND executing them on the target
  runtime, they worked, and they are therefore DELIBERATELY HELD at the versions
  the base commit declares.  That is why this region's task graph, its task
  names and its five artifact names are expected to be identical on both sides of
  the mirror: the tool that produces them was not replaced.  Bumping a component
  that had been proven to work would itself have breached the rule.
- Governing rule on the frontend dependency graph: the graph must be fully
  reproducible from a committed lockfile, with no dependency resolving
  differently between two checkouts.  Its bearing on this region is indirect but
  real - it is what makes the concatenation order that produces these artifacts
  stable across runs, and therefore what makes a byte comparison meaningful at
  all.  The lockfile itself is out of this region's territory: no lockfile
  content, no dependency specification and no dependency inventory appears here,
  and no dependency tree is listed.  Those belong to the migration's own
  registers.
- Governing rule on the asset-layout contract: the task graph, the asset globs
  and the five output artifact names are contract, and every dependency key is
  immutable because the asset resolver holds a long list of literal
  dependency-directory paths.  Verified at commit c8f6226105, the five names this
  region's pipeline produces are assets/dist/application.js,
  assets/dist/application.min.js, assets/dist/vendors.min.js,
  assets/dist/application.min.css and the rewritten home.html.  They are named
  here so the region can be read; their digests belong to artifacts/.
- Governing rule on namespace invariance: it is honoured here trivially and
  completely, in that this region names no package namespace at all and contains
  no reference to the namespace the migration excludes.  The excluded frontend
  technologies - a different frontend framework, a typed-JavaScript dialect, a
  module-bundling toolchain and any interface redesign - are likewise absent, and
  nothing here reads as advocacy for any of them.  The pipeline this region
  observes is the existing task-runner pipeline, unchanged.
- Governing rule that nothing is silently skipped: honoured by making this skip
  loud.  region-status is a fixed vocabulary of exactly CAPTURED or SKIPPED,
  skip-reason names the specific unmet preconditions and distinguishes them from
  one another, and the delimiters are present and empty rather than absent.  A
  reader or a script can tell in one line that this region carries no
  observation, which is the opposite of a silent skip.
- Governing rule against relying on JDK-internal access in production launch
  configuration.  Verified at the base commit and after migration: that
  configuration contains ZERO module-access JVM arguments.  Had such an argument
  appeared in a captured banner it would be replaced, in this region, by the
  description "a module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an
  otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module", and the substitution
  would be recorded in this notes block.  None arose, so none was made.  The
  register of applied exceptions is referred to BY TITLE ONLY, as
  the JDK access exceptions register, because its filename embeds a token this
  tree excludes; it is delivered empty with a positive statement that none are
  required.
- Registered observation, not a defect report against this file and not something
  to fix: the two recursive scans for module-access tokens that this deliverable
  is checked against cannot return zero across the whole documentation tree,
  because the register just referred to carries such a token inside its own
  required filename and several migration documents plus the top-level readme and
  the setup page cite it by that name.  Those files are outside this file's scope
  and were not modified.  Within docs/migration/smoke-evidence the count is zero,
  and this file keeps it zero.
- Provenance of the rules quoted above, and both facts belong together because
  either alone misleads.  FIRST, the project's rules facility reports, verbatim,
  "No user rules provided" - there is no on-disk user rules document for this
  project, and that is explicitly NOT licence to lower the bar; the work is held
  to enterprise-standard practice on top of the rules, never instead of them.
  SECOND, rules are nonetheless present and binding: the requirements embed an
  explicit, numbered block of seven rules that governs this work in full exactly
  as an external rules document would, alongside seven transformation rules.  The
  identifiers the migration plan uses for them are its OWN navigational
  convention rather than quoted titles, which is why every rule above is quoted
  or summarised by its text rather than cited by number alone.  No rule was
  invented and none was softened.
- Credential posture, in the repository's own voice rather than a policy invented
  here.  At the base commit docs/setup.md:78 states that real keystore and
  trust-store passwords must not be checked into source control and should
  instead be set through environment variables or a secret manager in any
  non-local environment, and that page already renders both values as <REDACTED>
  at :86 and :87 and the administrator password as <REDACTED> at :119, where the
  top-level readme does not.  This capture honours that posture and reuses that
  token.  Measured rather than asserted: the documented administrator password
  occurs at exactly ONE location in the whole tracked tree, in the top-level
  readme, and ZERO times anywhere under docs/migration - and this file keeps that
  count at zero by never transcribing it.
- The launch configuration audited for the rule on JDK-internal access is, at
  commit c8f6226105, the block at README.md:111-127, duplicated in docs/setup.md.
  It is described here and cited by anchor, and deliberately never reproduced
  verbatim, for two reasons: it carries credential-shaped values, and it also
  carries two pre-existing unbalanced quotation marks - confirmed by counting
  quotation marks per line, at README.md:119 and spanning README.md:123-124.
  Importing that block would copy a pre-existing defect into a new file for no
  benefit, and the rule on discovered defects forbids repairing it, so it is
  neither repaired nor imported.
- Registered observation, likewise not fixed: the current working tree has moved
  past the base commit, so every anchor in this file is cited as a BASE-COMMIT
  anchor and should be re-read at commit c8f6226105, which is the only revision
  at which a baseline is meaningful.
- expected-evidence, and every line so labelled is an EXPECTATION derived by
  reading the repository at commit c8f6226105, never an observation of a run.
  Nothing below was witnessed on this host.  The wiring anchors, the task graph,
  the forced-execution setting, the two masked aliases and the determinism
  findings are all true of the repository regardless of whether the capture ran,
  which is why they are recorded even though the region is empty - the mirror
  needs them to select the same region and to read it the same way.
- expected-evidence: this region exists at all because the asset pipeline runs
  INSIDE the deploying application at container startup, not at build time.  A
  search across every module build file for an asset-pipeline plugin, a
  runtime-version declaration or a pipeline invocation returns nothing; the
  pipeline is driven instead by a servlet-context-aware Spring bean, declared at
  spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:26 and implemented by
  AngularResourceCopier.java, whose class declaration at :79 carries that
  interface.  That is why the package-manager migration has a runtime-wiring
  component, and why a deployment log region is the ONLY place the startup-time
  pipeline is ever observed.
- expected-evidence: the capture precondition, restated as a mechanism because
  getting it wrong empties the region.  The helper at
  AngularResourceCopier.java:325-342 pipes the child process's standard output
  and standard error into the application's own logger at DEBUG level, through a
  stream handler at :338 wrapping a DEBUG-level output-stream adapter constructed
  at :335; the adapter was inspected directly and every relayed line goes out
  through the logger's debug method.  The invocation itself is announced at :327
  and the completion at :340, both also at DEBUG.  DEBUG must therefore be
  enabled for that logger or this region contains nothing but the two bracket
  lines - which is precisely condition (2) in skip-reason.
- expected-evidence: three invocation pairs, in this order, each appearing as an
  About to run [{}] line followed by a done with [{}]: exit code {} line.  ONE,
  the dependency install, at AngularResourceCopier.java:131, from the
  yarnInstallCommand property configured at
  spring-web-ark-angular-starter.xml:33-35.  TWO, the default asset task graph,
  at :168, from the gruntDefaultCommand property configured at :36-38.  THREE,
  the module-configuration merge, at :203, from the mergeConfigFrontendTask
  property configured at :28-30, which invokes the task-runner target registered
  at Gruntfile.js:183.
- expected-evidence: the three property KEYS above are the command identities the
  normalisation policy preserves, and all three are stable across the mirror -
  verified, the install property retains its original key in the migrated tree
  even though its VALUE is replaced.  A reader comparing the two sides should
  therefore expect the same three keys in the same order, with a changed value on
  the first pair only.  The key is cited because it is the stable handle; it is
  not an endorsement of the tool its name recalls.
- expected-evidence: a non-obvious property of the THIRD pair that a mechanical
  diff will otherwise trip over.  The call at :203 sits inside a per-profile
  helper invoked from the loop at :141-144, once for EACH active profile, and the
  active-profile list built at :135-137 is the extension profile when one is
  present plus a fixed custom profile.  The third pair therefore REPEATS, and its
  repetition count is an environment-dependent property of the capture rather
  than a defect.  A baseline and a migrated capture taken with different active
  profiles will differ in that count for a reason that carries no behavioural
  signal, so the profile set must match across the mirror.
- expected-evidence: the pre-migration package manager's install invocation is
  what this baseline region records for the FIRST pair.  It is named here only as
  the thing observed at the base commit; it is not a prerequisite and not a
  recommendation, and it is REPLACED in the migrated capture by a
  lockfile-respecting clean install.  The configured value at :34 carries
  skip-integrity-check, ignore-engines, no-progress and non-interactive flags,
  and it is the only one of the three commands that does not route through the
  task runner.
- expected-evidence: the default task graph is frozen contract.  Gruntfile.js:376
  registers it as exactly loadConfig, clean, ngAnnotate, uglify, concat, cssmin,
  renderHome, cacheBust, copyToModulesConfigFolder - nine tasks, in that order.
  Per-task progress lines are captured where the log carries them, with task
  names, graph order and per-task file counts preserved and the clock normalised.
  The graph produces the five artifacts named above.
- expected-evidence, determinism finding 1 of 4: cache busting is CONTENT-HASH
  based.  Its configuration at Gruntfile.js:79-86 rewrites the home page against
  the distribution directory, with no timestamp, no build banner and no date
  injection anywhere in the file - a search for banner, date-construction and
  template-timestamp constructs returns nothing at all.  Repeated builds of
  identical input therefore produce identical output.
- expected-evidence, determinism finding 2 of 4: minification runs with
  identifier mangling DISABLED, at Gruntfile.js:51, which removes the principal
  source of benign inter-build variance.
- expected-evidence, determinism finding 3 of 4: concatenation order derives from
  the frozen asset globs, so preserving every dependency key and the install
  topology means that order cannot drift.  The clean task also wipes the
  distribution directory first, configured at Gruntfile.js:131, so a stale
  artefact cannot masquerade as a match.
- expected-evidence, determinism finding 4 of 4, and it is an honest caveat
  stated rather than glossed: source-map generation is ENABLED, at
  Gruntfile.js:52, and a source map embeds file paths.  The comparison build must
  therefore execute from the SAME relative path, or the map file will differ
  while the JavaScript bundles do not.  The comparison is scoped to the five
  artifacts accordingly, and the map is treated as path-sensitive rather than
  behaviour-bearing.
- expected-evidence: an environment input that conditions the pipeline.  The
  development value of the node-environment variable is exported in the launch
  configuration - set at README.md:121 inside the block described above, and
  documented in four places across the readme and the setup page in the current
  tree.  It reaches exactly one branch: Gruntfile.js:163 tests the variable for
  the exact value production when the renderHome task decides which asset lists
  to render into the home page, so any other value, including an unset variable,
  follows the same development branch.  It is recorded as an input to this
  capture because it selects the content of one of the five artifacts.
- expected-evidence, stated as a fact about a MECHANISM and not as an action
  performed here: the generated configuration module is written into the working
  directory by AngularResourceCopier.java:206-215, with the exported shape built
  at :208-210 and the write performed at :212 with replace-existing semantics.
  Replace-existing is why a deployed environment continues to overwrite whatever
  default is present with its own value, and therefore why committing a tracked
  default cannot change deploy-time behaviour.  This file neither creates that
  module nor asks for it to be created; it records how the writer behaves.
- expected-evidence: warning and error lines from the pipeline are captured AS
  OBSERVED, with their message text preserved in full and nothing repaired or
  retried.  The consolidated error-severity scan is a separate sibling region and
  is not duplicated here.
- expected-evidence, and it is the sharpest consequence of the level established
  in skip-reason, so it is stated precisely rather than approximately.  The relay
  is uniformly at DEBUG: a child task's own warning and error TEXT reaches the log
  through the same adapter as its ordinary output, so it carries DEBUG severity
  regardless of how severe the message reads.  Counting the copier's own logging
  calls at commit c8f6226105 gives seventeen at DEBUG, eight at TRACE, one at INFO
  and one at ERROR, and NONE at WARN.  With the copier's logger pinned at WARN,
  therefore, the only line this class can contribute at all is the single ERROR at
  AngularResourceCopier.java:185, which fires only when the whole resource copy
  aborts on an input-output failure and then deliberately throws to stop the
  deployment.  Everything else - all three invocation announcements, every relayed
  line of pipeline output including its warnings, and all three completion lines -
  is discarded.  A broken asset build under that configuration leaves NO log trace
  whatsoever, which is a stronger statement than the evidence rule needs and the
  most direct possible argument for asserting on the artifacts instead.
- Mirror obligation.  The counterpart is migrated/startup/frontend-build.log: the
  same filename, the same field set in the same order including
  exit-status-is-not-evidence, and the same region-selection rule stated above, so
  that a recursive diff between the two capture directories is mechanically
  meaningful.  A field added on one side only, or a region selected differently on
  one side only, would register as a difference that carries no behavioural signal
  at all.
